# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine cloned.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true, so a clone made there rewrites
# every text file to CRLF on checkout. The files this repo SERVES are shell
# scripts that other machines fetch and run, and bash does not tolerate CRLF:
# it fails with `set: pipefail: invalid option name` and `$'\r': command not
# found`, naming neither the file nor the cause.
#
# Measured 2026-09-20 on a Windows checkout of a consuming repo -- every .sh
# was CRLF and none could run, while the same commit on Linux was LF and fine.
# A repo whose product is scripts served over HTTP cannot let the cloner's
# platform decide their bytes, so `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf rather than
# asking it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
